# sentinel — the operator's file. Copy to .env on Storage-NAS and edit.
#
# deploy.sh creates .env from this file ONCE, on first deploy, and
# never overwrites it afterwards. That is deliberate: several of these values are
# judgements made against the fleet as it is (the two media paths, the ssh key
# path, the bind address), and a deploy that reset them would silently revert a
# correction. `--status` reports which of these are still at their defaults.
#
# TWO VALUES HERE ARE SENSITIVE: the write token below, and SENTINEL_LLM_KEY in
# the advisor section. Both are credentials; everything else is configuration.
# The ssh key is a FILE mounted into the container, not a value in this file.
# This file is mode 600 on the NAS for those two values' sake; do not commit a
# populated copy.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paths on Storage-NAS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where this stack's own directory lives. The SQLite store goes in ./data under
# it. Must be on the NAS's own filesystem, never a network share.
MONITOR_DATA=/share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/Programs/sentinel/data

# The pvr stack's media directory. recordings and transcoded are the SAME
# filesystem -- a full volume stops recording and transcoding together, which is
# why the dashboard treats it as one critical metric rather than two.
PVR_DATA=/share/CACHEDEV1_DATA/Programs/pvr

# THE PATHS INSIDE THE CONTAINER ARE NOT THE PATHS ON THE HOST, so these two
# must be set explicitly. The compose file mounts the tree above at /mnt/, and  # secretscan:ignore (measured path, documented in this file)
# config.py's defaults are the HOST paths, which do not exist in here. Leaving
# these at their defaults is the kind of mistake that produces a permanent "the
# media volume does not exist" RED on a fleet whose media is fine.
RECORDINGS_PATH=/mnt/recordings
TRANSCODED_PATH=/mnt/transcoded

TZ=Europe/Helsinki


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dashboard
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The container's OWN LAN address on the `eth1` macvlan network, matching how
# every other service on Storage-NAS is addressed. The dashboard is reached at
# http://${LOCAL_IPV4}:8787 and the container publishes NO host port.
#
# .51 IS DELIBERATELY A NEW ALLOCATION, not a reuse. Checked free 2026-09-26:
# no ping reply and no ARP entry, and it appears in no reservation. The
# neighbours are .49 (the host's eth1 services address), .50 (host ssh/smb),
# .52/.53/.54/.56/.59/.61/.62/.63/.70 (live containers), .55 and .58 (reserved
# for hermes/nginx), .60 (Backup-NAS) and .64-.95 (Docker's DHCP pool, which
# must not be touched). .57 is labelled openclaw but openclaw runs on a bridge,
# so it was passed over: a name-reserved address can collide the day its owner
# actually claims it.
LOCAL_IPV4=198.51.100.11
MONITOR_PORT=8787

# How often the whole fleet is collected. 60 s is the design point: the fleet's
# fastest-changing signals (a pass starting, a tuner event) are minute-scale, and
# a shorter interval would mostly add load to Backup-NAS, which has 57 MB free
# and a load average of 2.2 on one core.
MONITOR_INTERVAL=60

# Client-side ssh bound, per call. This is a REAL bound, not a hint: the CuBoxes'
# state mount is mounted HARD, so a stat against a dead Backup-NAS blocks in
# D state, which no server-side timeout and no signal can kill. Raising this
# slows the fleet's discovery of a dead host; lowering it below ~10 s starts
# producing timeouts on a merely busy box, which read as UNKNOWN.
MONITOR_SSH_TIMEOUT=25

# Where command resource locks live. A one-shot `--run` is a new process, so the
# locks are flock files, not in-memory state: two invocations must not be able to
# own one serial adapter. Must be writable by every process that dispatches.
#MONITOR_LOCK_DIR=/tmp/sentinel-locks

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The write token (the settings page and PUT/DELETE on /api/v1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reads are open on the LAN (the existing bypass). WRITES ARE NOT: every
# `PUT/DELETE /api/v1/modules/...` must carry `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
# The SPA asks for the token once per browser session and keeps it in
# sessionStorage, never localStorage.
#
# Unset means writes are DISABLED -- the API answers 403 `writes_disabled`, so a
# deployment that was never given a token cannot be written to by anyone who
# guesses that the header is optional. The token is compared in constant time,
# is never stored in the database, returned by a read, or logged, and is a
# RESERVED key no module may declare.
#
# Generate one (64 hex characters):
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# then add the line, mode 600 on the NAS's copy, and restart the container.
# Rotating it invalidates every browser session; the page stays readable and
# re-opens its unlock dialog on the next write.
#SENTINEL_WRITE_TOKEN=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Module selection
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Modules are discovered by package under app/modules/. Unset means every
# discovered module. SENTINEL_MODULES NARROWS the set; a selector naming an
# unknown id is a startup error, because silently enabling only what it can find
# would read as a healthy fleet.
#
# A per-module `<ID>_ENABLED=0` disables one without editing the selector. A
# disabled module is not preloaded, its commands refuse, and its live incidents
# are FROZEN every epoch (UNKNOWN, never resolved). An unset key means enabled: a
# missing env var must never silently turn a module off.
#
# EVERY MODULE SETTING BELOW CAN ALSO BE SET FROM THE SETTINGS PAGE. The
# precedence is UI (a DB override) > this file > the module declaration's
# default, and a saved value applies on the NEXT COLLECTION -- no restart. A
# line here is therefore the FALLBACK, not the final word: `GET /api/v1/settings`
# reports which layer won (`source: db|env|default`), and DELETE on the key
# restores this file's value.
#
# MODULE ENABLEMENT IS DB-WRITABLE TOO: `PUT /api/v1/modules/<id>/enabled`
# writes `<ID>_ENABLED`, and the DB override wins. Once the settings page
# disables a module, editing `FLEET_ENABLED` here does NOT re-enable it -- there
# is no DELETE/reset path for that key, only the enable endpoint. Of the module
# settings only SENTINEL_MODULES stays file-only; the write token and every
# MONITOR_* platform key are file-only too.
#SENTINEL_MODULES=fleet
#FLEET_ENABLED=1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# System resources (module `system`) -- the host the monitor runs on
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reads the monitor container's own /proc and /sys once per epoch: load average,
# memory, free space on SYSTEM_MOUNTS, /proc/mdstat and the thermal zones. No
# ssh, no network, no writes. Its checks report under the target `local`.
#
# SYSTEM_MOUNTS is a comma-separated list. The mount with the LOWEST free
# percentage is graded; the incident subject is always the constant `disk` and
# every mount's figure (plus the worst one) is carried in the detail/evidence.
# A configured path that is not a MOUNT is unreadable, not a reading: stat would
# otherwise grade its parent filesystem. Any unreadable mount makes the check
# UNKNOWN, naming it. A host without /proc/mdstat or without a thermal zone
# reports UNKNOWN with the reason -- never green.
#SYSTEM_PROC=/proc
#SYSTEM_SYS=/sys
#SYSTEM_MOUNTS=/
#SYSTEM_ENABLED=1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network devices (module `snmp`) -- SNMP v2c, read-only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One row per device in SNMP_DEVICES, one row per polled OID in SNMP_CHECKS,
# and a SNMP_COMMUNITIES map keyed by device name. All three are single-line
# JSON values; the settings page edits the same settings.
#
# COMMUNITIES ARE A SEPARATE, WRITE-ONLY SETTING. They are a secret value: the
# settings API never returns the map, the audit log redacts it, and a blank
# value on save keeps the stored one. A community belongs to exactly one
# device name, and a device with no entry is reported unreachable rather than
# polled with a blank credential.
#
# The module issues GET requests only -- no writes, no walks -- and refuses any
# device whose version is not v2c. A device with no checks is still polled for
# reachability (sysUpTime.0), so an entry here is never silently unmonitored.
# `kind` is `gauge` (green/amber band) or `state` (numeric equality with
# `expected`; ifOperStatus up is 1). The OID is dotted-numeric; the leading
# display dot is accepted and stripped.
#
# SNMP_TIMEOUT_S bounds one request (at least 1); the client retries once.
# The addresses and community below are PLACEHOLDERS -- replace them.
#SNMP_DEVICES=[{"name":"switch-1","address":"192.0.2.200","port":161,"version":"v2c","enabled":true}]
#SNMP_COMMUNITIES={"switch-1":"change-me"}
#SNMP_CHECKS=[{"device":"switch-1","oid":"1.3.6.1.2.1.2.2.1.8.1","label":"Port 1 status","unit":"","direction":"high_is_good","green":0,"amber":0,"kind":"state","expected":1}]
#SNMP_TIMEOUT_S=3
#SNMP_ENABLED=1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the monitor may DO, as opposed to what it may see
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Remedies are TIER 2 ONLY: a remedy runs only when every precondition is
# POSITIVELY observed, and any `unknown` in that set means no remedy. There are
# deliberately very few, and the ones that exist are documented in
# docs/architecture.md along with the ones that were removed and why --
# a `rm -f *.part` sweep and a restart-on-NRestarts were both designed, then
# deleted, because each reproduced the silent failure it was written to cure.
#
# THREE SWITCHES, ALL REQUIRED, and none of them is redundant:
#   MONITOR_REMEDIES      process kill switch, read before the engine starts
#   SENTINEL_REMEDIATION  engine master switch, enforced by the ladder
#   <MODULE>_AUTHORITY    the per-module level; `off` by default
# A container that upgrades must not start acting because a new build shipped
# the engine, so every module still has to be named.
#
# Leaving remedies off is a valid permanent choice: the dashboard still shows
# every incident, and every remedy that would have run is still described.
MONITOR_REMEDIES=0
#SENTINEL_REMEDIATION=1

# The authority ladder, per module. `off` (the default), `advisory` (surface
# only), `propose` (write a pending proposal; a human approves), `scoped`
# (execute only the ALLOW list), `full` (any declared action, but a destructive
# one still needs its own switch).
#FLEET_AUTHORITY=off
#FLEET_ALLOW=
#FLEET_ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE=0

# One action, up or down, without widening the module:
#  <MODULE>_ACTION_<ACTION_ID>_AUTHORITY=full|scoped|propose|advisory|off
# For example, the fleet's two declared actions are `state_mount_cycle` and
# `transcode_restart`:
#FLEET_ACTION_STATE_MOUNT_CYCLE_AUTHORITY=off
#FLEET_ACTION_TRANSCODE_RESTART_AUTHORITY=off

# Collect and evaluate but write NOTHING to the store -- not a single row, not
# even a check outcome. Used for the first run against a live fleet, and for
# testing a threshold change without leaving a false pass record behind (item 55:
# the mode whose contract is "claims nothing" has to actually claim nothing).
MONITOR_DRY_RUN=0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Advisors -- read-only statistical advice, ON BY DEFAULT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SENTINEL_ADVISORS names the advisors to run each epoch: a comma-separated
# list of `module.advisor` or bare `advisor` ids, matched against the enabled
# modules plus the platform's own advisors.
#
#   unset / empty            -> the reference advisor runs (the default)
#   SENTINEL_ADVISORS=none   -> NO advisor runs (the explicit off switch)
#   a list                   -> exactly those; the default reference advisor
#                               does NOT run unless it is named
#
# The reference advisor is ADVISORY ONLY. Each epoch it summarises the window's
# incidents and, when a metric's p95 over the window is outside its threshold
# band (with at least 7 days of history, 20 samples, and a sample span covering
# the window), emits an insight naming the current band and a proposed one. The
# proposal moves only the failing edge (amber) toward the observation, by at
# most 25% of the band width; green never moves. It never writes a proposal and
# never changes a threshold; a human decision is the only route to a change.
#
#SENTINEL_ADVISORS=none
#
# The cap on history any advisor may read, in days. The reference advisor asks
# for 7 and refuses to suggest a band from a shorter window. A negative value
# refuses to start rather than clamping to "no history".
#SENTINEL_ADVISOR_MAX_HISTORY_DAYS=30
#
# One advisor's own ask, overriding its built-in default (the reference asks
# for 7). The key is SENTINEL_ADVISOR_<ID>_HISTORY_DAYS, with the id uppercased
# and every non-alphanumeric character replaced by `_`: `fleet.drift` becomes
# SENTINEL_ADVISOR_FLEET_DRIFT_HISTORY_DAYS. IT IS STILL CAPPED by the value
# above -- it is the advisor's request, not permission to exceed the cap. Zero
# or negative refuses rather than becoming a window nobody asked for.
#SENTINEL_ADVISOR_REFERENCE_HISTORY_DAYS=7


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Proposals -- the operator approval queue
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A `propose`-level action (or an advice-driven threshold change) is written as
# a pending proposal instead of running. One pending proposal exists per
# (incident, action); the engine does not duplicate it every epoch.
#
# This is how long an operator has to decide, in HOURS. An expired proposal is
# shown as EXPIRED, never reused, and cannot be approved. Zero or negative
# refuses to start, because it would mean every proposal is born expired.
#SENTINEL_PROPOSAL_TTL_H=24


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The LLM advisor -- OFF BY DEFAULT, and an egress opt-in
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONFIGURING THE ENDPOINT MEANS DATA LEAVES THIS CONTAINER. The advisor asks
# the snapshot for the window it is given -- incident summaries, metric series,
# journal lines -- and that material would travel to whatever endpoint is named
# below. The transmission is the operator's decision, not a default: leave these
# empty and nothing is ever sent anywhere.
#
# The advisor is REGISTERED only when provider AND endpoint are both set, and
# it RUNS only when named in SENTINEL_ADVISORS (e.g. `SENTINEL_ADVISORS=llm` or
# `platform.llm`). Configuring it never adds it to the default selector, which
# stays `reference`.
#
# The transport is not implemented in this delivery: the seam is configured here
# ahead of it, and a configured+selected advisor records an explicit "not
# implemented" error row rather than making a network call. Do not put a real
# key here until that follow-up lands.
#
# SENTINEL_LLM_KEY IS A SECRET. It is read from this file (whose mode is what
# protects it) and is never written to the store, a log line or --showconf.
#SENTINEL_LLM_PROVIDER=
#SENTINEL_LLM_ENDPOINT=
#SENTINEL_LLM_KEY=
#SENTINEL_LLM_MODEL=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The fleet
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which boxes this module watches. Each id gets a per-box check expansion and its
# own incident subject; a box listed here with no host entry is reported as not
# monitored rather than silently skipped.
#CUBOX_IDS=cubox-1,cubox-2

# The host the monitor RUNS ON. Its checks are local (paths, docker socket), and
# "unreachable" is not a thing that can happen to it. This is data, not a
# platform constant, so the monitor does not assume it runs on the NAS it watches.
#MONITOR_LOCAL_HOST=storage

# Both CuBoxes, and the two hosts they depend on. Addresses rather than ssh
# aliases: there is no ~/.ssh/config in the container, and an alias that resolves
# on the operator's laptop and not in here is a failure that only appears after
# deployment. THE DEFAULTS IN THE CODE ARE RFC 5737 PLACEHOLDERS: set every one.
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_1_ADDR=198.51.100.31
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_1_USER=root
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_2_ADDR=198.51.100.32
#MONITOR_HOST_CUBOX_2_USER=root
#MONITOR_HOST_BACKUP_ADDR=198.51.100.20
#MONITOR_HOST_BACKUP_USER=admin

# TVH's own API base, for `tvh_response_ms`. A deployment value like the host
# addresses; the code default is a documentation-range placeholder.
#TVH_URL=http://198.51.100.12:9981

# Advanced path overrides, only if the NAS layout differs from the measured one.
# The defaults are beside the fleet module and are the paths the checks were
# captured against; repointing one without repointing the others is how a check
# starts grading a directory that does not exist.
#CUBPXE_ROOT=/share/HDA_DATA/cubpxe  # secretscan:ignore (measured path, documented in this file)
#STATE_EXPORT_BASE=/share/HDA_DATA/cubpxe/state  # secretscan:ignore (measured path, documented in this file)
#NFSROOT_PATH=/share/HDA_DATA/cubpxe/nfsroot  # secretscan:ignore (measured path, documented in this file)
#TFTP_ROOT=/share/HDA_DATA/Public/cubpxe-boot  # secretscan:ignore (measured path, documented in this file)
#EXPORTS_FILE=/etc/exports
#EXPORT_NAME=/share/HDA_DATA/cubpxe  # secretscan:ignore (measured path, documented in this file)
#TFTP_FILES=zImage,initrd.img,imx6q-cubox-i.dtb
#DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock

# The TVH container, as `docker ps` reports it, and the DVB adapters the
# WinTV-dualHD exposes. NOTE the second tuner currently refuses recordings
# (operator-reported): that is modelled as a KNOWN-ACCEPTED condition with a
# dated acknowledgement, not as a fault, because a check that is RED from day one
# trains the operator to ignore red. Presence is checked; function is only
# asserted when a real recording has happened.
TVH_CONTAINER=tvheadend
DVB_ADAPTERS=adapter0,adapter1

# How much of the TVH container log one collection reads. This is a WINDOW SIZE,
# not a detail: the DVR-pairing check pairs a recording's subscribe against its
# unsubscribe, so a tail shorter than one recording cannot see both ends and must
# report UNKNOWN rather than green.
TVH_LOG_TAIL=20000


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TVH's own API -- OPTIONAL, and currently INERT. Read the whole note.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not needed for the DVB checks: the container log carries adapter and DVR
# events, and that is where they are read from.
#
# NOTHING READS THESE YET, SO SETTING THEM CHANGES NO VERDICT. Only `TVH_USER`
# is parsed (into `cfg.tvh_user`); `TVH_PASS` is read by no code at all -- it is
# a placeholder for the authentication probe described below, and setting it is
# currently a no-op. The only consumer of `tvh_user` is `tvh_response_ms`, which
# records `auth_configured: bool(cfg.tvh_user)` in its EVIDENCE and never
# authenticates with it -- `probes.http_probe()` takes no credentials at all.
# Verified 2026-10-20 against the module tree.
# This note previously said both were parsed, and before that promised
# "per-tuner signal quality", which was never implemented; an operator who set
# these expecting a change would find none and no explanation for it.
#
# What they CANNOT fix, and why the 401 is not a defect. TVH has auth on, so
# `tvh_response_ms` gets 401 and reports UNKNOWN -- deliberately, because `curl
# -f` would make that a PERMANENT RED while "any HTTP code came back" would make
# it a permanent GREEN on a service that answered nothing. Note also that a
# WRONG password returns the same 401 as NO password, so "the credentials work"
# needs its own validity probe and must never be inferred from an answer.
#
# A consequence worth knowing before you wait for it: UNKNOWN does not resolve an
# incident (store.py -- only a positive observation does), so a TVH incident
# opened while it was RED stays open for as long as this check stays grey, and
# no configuration closes it. That is deliberate.
#
# To use these, extend the probe to authenticate AND distinguish "authenticated,
# real status" from "401, wrong-or-absent password". Until then: leave them
# commented out.
#TVH_USER=
#TVH_PASS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The CuBox serial consoles
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The FT230X chip serial of each box's console adapter. THESE ARE DEPLOYMENT VALUES,
# NOT SECRETS AND NOT CODE: read each one on Storage-NAS from
#
#     for t in /sys/class/tty/ttyUSB*; do
#         p=$(readlink -f $t/device)
#         while [ "$p" != "/" ] && [ ! -f "$p/serial" ]; do p=$(dirname "$p"); done
#         echo "$(basename $t) -> $(cat $p/serial)"
#     done
#
# and put the real value in .env, which is gitignored. Do NOT commit them: a chip
# serial names a specific piece of the operator's hardware, and every file in the
# repo is published.
#
# They identify the console ADAPTER, which is what lets sentinel tell the two boxes
# apart when the kernel swaps ttyUSB0 and ttyUSB1 -- the numbers are assigned in USB
# enumeration order, so a reboot or a re-plug can exchange them. Getting this wrong
# does not merely mislabel a dashboard row: it sends a SysRq BREAK to the wrong box.
#
# Left commented on purpose. config.py treats an unset serial as UNCONFIGURED, which
# is a distinct and visible state; a default here would be a guess, and a guess is
# exactly what the resolver refuses to make.
#SERIAL_CUBOX_1_SERIAL=
#SERIAL_CUBOX_2_SERIAL=
#
# Where arm files and capture logs live, and the ceiling on an arm window.
# Commented so the defaults (the serial monitoring design, section 7) apply:
# a stale arm holds the port and blocks a BREAK, so it must expire.
#SERIAL_CAPTURE_DIR=/data/serial
#SERIAL_ARM_MAX_MIN=30
#
# Whether a missing adapter is a fault. Default 0: the adapter is an operator
# tool, and a permanent alarm on a healthy fleet trains the operator to ignore
# red. Set to 1 only if one is wired permanently.
#SERIAL_REQUIRED=0
#
# After a NAS reboot the serial modules are re-applied by a five-minute watchdog
# cron, not a boot hook, so there is a window in which nothing is bound. The
# adapter check reports UNKNOWN across it rather than FAIL.
#SERIAL_WATCHDOG_GRACE_S=360
#
# The device NUMBER is a read-only fallback only, never an identity: any action
# that writes (a BREAK) requires a resolved chip serial.
#SERIAL_DEVICE=/dev/ttyUSB0
#SERIAL_BAUD=115200
# Override only for a test harness with a fake sysfs/proc tree.
#SERIAL_SYSFS=/sys
#SERIAL_PROC=/proc
